so in and et al., et al., 2024). Specifically, more scalable and phenotyping help the at which has et al., 2023). environmental data and sensor networks further enhance the predictive of breeding models. refining ML algorithms for help shift the from predictive performance to also the and (e.g., et al., and help advances in computational capabilities with phenotyping and market in the global et al., which is essential for developing environmentally resilient and sustainable crops where they are et al., on standardized data and robust scalable computing and data and to AI tools on interpretability (XAI) than predictive power already enhance while and datasets and pipelines et al., accelerate model and in breeding integration of machine learning with high-throughput phenotypic and genotypic data is plant genetics and crop breeding (Schrider and Kern, is increasingly recognized that global food is both by and to environmental shifts et al., 2020). These technologies help the for varieties capable of withstanding diverse environmental while maintaining high enabling selection across a of and ML and AI improve the efficiency of superior genotypes. AI and ML tools more and training datasets their in crop programs is to (Varshney, 2021), supporting sustainable and global food security (Benitez-Alfonso et al., 2023). data and be essential to these technologies et al., the between et al., 2025), computational and et al., 2024) also be essential for the potential of machine learning to enhance plant breeding environmental conditions and market is both vulnerable to and on environmental conditions, integrated ML/AI pipelines combining and with by breeders and toward sustainable productivity and remains essential for achieving","doi":"10.3389/fpls.2026.1869724","year":"2026","venue":"Frontiers in Plant Science","sources":["openalex"]},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000053","candidate_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000053","source_record_id":"rec_000053","source_corpus":"update_2026-08-09","title":"Interpretable prediction of DNA replication origins in S. cerevisiae using DNABERT and DNABERT-2","abstract":"BACKGROUND: DNA replication is a biological process in which a single DNA molecule is duplicated, initiating from multiple genomic sites known as replication origins. Identifying replication origins and analyzing their underlying base sequence composition is crucial for understanding the mechanisms of DNA replication. Although there are various machine learning and deep learning approaches for origin prediction, many rely on labor intensive feature engineering or lack interpretability. We fine-tune two genome-based pretrained language models, DNABERT and DNABERT-2, to predict replication origins in budding yeast and unravel the DNA base composition behind them. The key contribution of this study is a systematic framework for analyzing genomic language models for replication origin prediction, combining controlled dataset design with model-specific explainability pipelines to examine how different tokenization strategies influence learned sequence features and whether such approaches can highlight biologically meaningful signals. RESULTS: We evaluate both models on the designed datasets to ensure robustness and support explainability. DNABERT demonstrates consistent performance, achieving an average accuracy of 0.72 for more challenging and 0.83 for the easier dataset. In comparison, DNABERT-2 achieved comparable scores of 0.72 and 0.81 on the same datasets. Our attention-based motif discovery pipeline enhances the interpretability of DNABERT, by identifying motifs from high-attention fragments that closely match known sequence patterns of replication origins. Perturbation-based explanation methods, including Shapley additive explanations, were applied to interpret DNABERT-2's learning mechanism. This analysis identified tokens with high attribution scores aligned with biologically relevant sequence composition. CONCLUSION: Our study demonstrates that both models identify replication origin sequences, albeit through different learning strategies. Tokenization appears to influence model learning and attention behavior in these models. The overlapping k-mer tokenization used in DNABERT yields more interpretable attention maps compared to the byte pair encoding tokenization employed in DNABERT-2. We show that despite sharing the same BERT-style architecture, DNABERT captures relevant short-range patterns and some sequence dependencies beyond just local context, as reflected in its attention maps. In contrast, DNABERT-2's alternative tokenization strategy biases its learning toward relevant short-range patterns by optimizing token weighting.","doi":"10.1186/s12859-026-06562-5","year":"2026","venue":"BMC Bioinformatics","sources":["openalex","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000054","candidate_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000054","source_record_id":"rec_000054","source_corpus":"update_2026-08-09","title":"Artificial intelligence in the early diagnosis of prostate cancer: from multimodal imaging to liquid biopsy","abstract":"Prostate cancer (PCa) is the most prevalent malignant tumor in the urogenital system among men worldwide. Due to its subtle early symptoms and strong tumor heterogeneity, traditional diagnostic methods relying on a single prostate-specific antigen (PSA) initial screening and subjective imaging evaluations often lead to high false positives, overt biopsies, and missed small lesions. The rapid development of artificial intelligence (AI) provides innovative solutions to overcome these clinical bottlenecks. This article comprehensively reviews the application of AI in the early intelligent diagnosis of PCa. In the fields of ultrasound, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and positron emission tomography/computed tomography (PET/CT) imaging, AI significantly enhances the accuracy of target lesion identification. It achieves this by deep decoding high-dimensional quantitative features and effectively reducing subjective bias. In non-invasive liquid biopsy, AI-driven multi-omics networks have successfully addressed challenging screening blind spots, such as the PSA gray zone. In light of current challenges such as limited model generalization capability and the \"black box effect\" of algorithms, this article looks forward to the development prospects of constructing multimodal fusion models based on federated learning and explainable AI (XAI), aiming to promote the transition of PCa diagnosis and treatment from algorithm development to real clinical decision support.","doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1906810","year":"2026","venue":"Frontiers in Oncology","sources":["openalex","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000055","candidate_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000055","source_record_id":"rec_000055","source_corpus":"update_2026-08-09","title":"A Systematic Review: Deep Learning for Analyzing Genomic Data to Discover Evolutionary Patterns","abstract":"Deep learning has been increasingly applied to evolutionary genomics as genomic datasets have grown in scale and complexity. However, the literature encompasses heterogeneous biological objectives, modeling assumptions, and evaluation standards, often treated as a unified field despite important conceptual differences. This study presents a systematic review of research published between 2016 and 2025 on the use of deep learning to identify evolutionary patterns in genomic data. Following a structured screening process, 50 studies were selected for qualitative synthesis. The reviewed applications can be organized into three partially overlapping but conceptually distinct domains: (i) population genetic inference, (ii) phylogenetic reconstruction, and (iii) sequence representation learning using DNA and protein language models. In population genetics, deep learning is predominantly employed within simulation‐based inference frameworks. In phylogenetics, neural architectures are used to approximate or accelerate tree and model inference under defined conditions. In representation learning, models focus on extracting transferable sequence features for downstream evolutionary or functional analyses. Across domains, deep learning provides flexible modeling of complex genomic inputs. Nevertheless, recurring limitations include challenges in interpretability, sensitivity to training assumptions—particularly under simulation‐based settings—heterogeneous evaluation protocols, and substantial computational demands. By organizing the literature using a domain‐aligned framework, this review clarifies domain‐specific strengths, limitations, and research gaps, providing a structured basis for future methodological development in evolutionary genomics.","doi":"10.1155/sci5/4286814","year":"2026","venue":"PubMed Central","sources":["openalex"]},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000056","candidate_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000056","source_record_id":"rec_000056","source_corpus":"update_2026-08-09","title":"A benchmark study of vision and pathology foundation models for computational pathology","abstract":"To advance precision medicine in pathology, artificial intelligence (AI)-driven foundation models must generalize across diverse datasets, tissues, and clinical tasks. However, their comparative performance and generalizability in computational pathology remain incompletely characterized. Here, we benchmark 32 AI foundation models across four categories, including general vision models (VM), general vision-language models (VLM), pathology-specific vision models (Path-VM), and pathology-specific vision-language models (Path-VLM), using slide- and patch-level tasks from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA), Clinical Proteomic Tumor Analysis Consortium (CPTAC), external benchmarking datasets, and out-of-domain datasets. Across TCGA tasks, Path-VMs consistently rank among the strongest performers. Evaluation across CPTAC and out-of-domain datasets reveals more nuanced generalization behavior, with model rankings showing modest but consistent shifts across datasets and task categories. Pairwise statistical comparisons indicate that differences among top-performing models are often small and task dependent. Path-VMs outperform Path-VLMs and remain competitive with VMs. Model size and pretraining dataset scale do not consistently predict downstream performance. Finally, late decision-level ensembling improves aggregate performance across external datasets and tissue types, highlighting complementary strengths across foundation models. PathBench: https://pathbench.stanford.edu/ The comparative performance and generalisability of pathology foundation models remain largely unexamined. Here, the authors benchmark 32 AI pathology foundation models across large cancer datasets, showing that generalisation in computational pathology is heterogeneous and task-dependent regardless of dataset scale, but ensemble-based approaches can combine the strengths of different models.","doi":"10.1038/s41467-026-76004-6","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Communications","sources":["openalex"]}]
./data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/04_abstract_screening_invalidated_configurable_runner_2026-08-10/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0007.stderr.log:109:{"results":[{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000049","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A new protein language model for protein-protein interactions is presented, but the abstract only discusses proteins and sequence/pairwise residue representations. No natural-language text component or text-bio bridge is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"protein language model ... model the evolution of protein-protein interactions\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000050","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary multimodal prediction study using neuroimaging and genetic data. The abstract does not describe any natural-language text component or text-to-bio mapping.","evidence_snippet":"\"multimodal neuroimaging and genetic data for the prediction of cognitive domains\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000051","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes an integrated analysis platform built around PlaqueNet for plaque classification and proteomic workflows. This is a downstream application/system rather than a primary text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrated system featuring 'PlaqueNet' ... automated workflows\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000052","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly says Editorial, and the abstract summarizes a research topic rather than presenting a new model. It is not a primary candidate model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"Editorial: Utilizing machine learning with phenotypic and genotypic data\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000053","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is about genome-based pretrained models on DNA replication origins. DNA tokenization is biological sequence modeling, not a natural-language text component or substantive text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"fine-tune two genome-based pretrained language models, DNABERT and DNABERT-2\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000054","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This article explicitly says it comprehensively reviews AI applications for prostate cancer diagnosis. It is a review, not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"This article comprehensively reviews the application of AI\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000055","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract states that this is a systematic review of deep learning studies. It synthesizes literature rather than presenting a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This study presents a systematic review of research\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000056","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This paper benchmarks 32 foundation models and PathBench rather than proposing a new primary model. It does include vision-language models evaluated on pathology tasks, but the paper is a benchmark resource.","evidence_snippet":"\"benchmark 32 AI foundation models ... including general vision-language models\""}]}
./data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/04_abstract_screening_invalidated_configurable_runner_2026-08-10/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0009.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000065","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Editorial overview of imaging and omics methods, not a primary model paper. It discusses foundation models in general but does not present a candidate text-bio generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Editorial: Methods for imaging and omics data science\"; \"This collection is the third and concluding installment of a trilogy\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000066","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of peptide cancer vaccine AI methods, not an original model paper. The abstract summarizes prediction tasks and architectures rather than proposing a new text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"We summarize the current role of AI across the peptide cancer vaccine development pipeline\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000067","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Primary model paper on genomic language models and paired genome-transcriptome data, but the model uses biological sequences rather than natural-language text. No substantive text-bio bridge is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"utilizes gLMs and sequence-to-function (S2F) embeddings\"; \"paired genome-transcriptome data\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000068","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A review of antimicrobial resistance prediction methods rather than a primary candidate model. The abstract focuses on sequence and multimodal learning for AMR, not natural-language text.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review systematically summarizes the research progress\"; \"protein language models\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000069","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract reads as a review/perspective on AI virtual cells and generative biomedical agents, not a primary model report. It mentions large language models, but the candidate text-bio model is not clearly specified.","evidence_snippet":"\"with the introduction of generative biomedical agents\"; \"leveraging the logical reasoning capabilities of large language models\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000070","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a critical review of generative AI for rice advisory systems, not an original model paper. The abstract references natural-language advisory and vision-language models, but the proposed candidate model is not clearly presented.","evidence_snippet":"\"The work presents GenAI as a transformative interface\"; \"through natural language\"; \"vision-language models\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000071","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a scoping review of AI in rare neurological diseases, not a primary model paper. It mentions foundation models and large language models, but does not clearly describe a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"a scoping review of the evidence landscape\"; \"foundation models and large language models\""},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000072","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Primary model paper on forest-tree genomic sequences and conditional DNA generation. The abstract is about biological sequence modeling, not natural-language text, so the text-bio bridge is absent.","evidence_snippet":"\"tailored for forest tree genomic sequences\"; \"de novo generation of synthetic DNA sequences\""}]}
./data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/04_abstract_screening_invalidated_configurable_runner_2026-08-10/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0011.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000081","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a web-based TCM platform/resource with bilingual terminology, ontology, and LLM-assisted normalization, not a primary model paper. The abstract emphasizes database and API layers rather than a new generative text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“comprehensive web-based platform”; “Bilingual TCM Corpus”; “LLM-assisted pipeline that converts all raw dosage expressions”"},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000082","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary transfer-learning framework for scRNA-seq that combines RNA expression with language-model embeddings. The text/language component is part of the model, not a side resource or benchmark.","evidence_snippet":"“combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models”"},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000083","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is a review that summarizes experimental and computational approaches. It does not present a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“This review summarizes experimental and computational approaches”; “we further discuss computational integration strategies”"},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000084","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract frames the work as a review and conceptual framework proposal rather than a primary model paper. Although it discusses LLM-based agents for multi-omics and cancer risk, the record is still a review/editorial type.","evidence_snippet":"“This review aimed to… propose a conceptual framework”; “Each agent uses a large language model as the computational backbone”"},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000085","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract states this is a systematic review of prior AI-ECG and multimodal studies. It does not describe a new text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“This systematic review examines recent advances”; “we analyzed 65 peer-reviewed studies”"},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000086","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary synthetic-biology method paper, but the language-model use is on protein sequences rather than natural-language text. No substantive text-bio bridge is described.","evidence_snippet":"“protein language models” ; “characterizing cellular responses to gas vesicle expression using RNA-seq”"},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000087","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"no","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The record is a position/research-gap note and explicitly says it establishes no empirical result. It does not present a candidate biological text model.","evidence_snippet":"“POSITION / RESEARCH-GAP NOTE”; “This note establishes no empirical result”"},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000088","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a benchmarking study of existing pathology foundation models rather than a new primary model paper. The text-image alignment aspect belongs to the evaluated models, not a new candidate model in this paper.","evidence_snippet":"“Benchmarking Open-Source Pathology Foundation Models”; “Transformer-based Pathology Image and Text Alignment Network”"}]}
